    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n73492" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ὅμηρος</span></b> , ὁ, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>Homer ;</i> the name first occurs
			in <i><span class="en">
				Hes.
				<u>Fr.</u>
				265.1
			</span></i> (dub.), <i><span class="en">
				Xenoph.
				10
			</span></i>,<i><span class="en">
				11
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Hdt.
				2.53
			</span></i> : <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὅμηρος</span> was a Cumaean
			word for <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τυφλός</span> acc. to Ps.-<i><span class="en">
				Hdt.
				<u>Vit.Hom.</u>
				13
			</span></i>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Lyc.
				422
			</span></i> ; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὁμηρεύω</span> was 
				Ion.
			 for <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἡγοῦμαι</span>
			<i>guide the blind,</i> acc. to Ephor.I<i><span class="en">
				J.
			</span></i> ; but <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ὅμαρος</span> is a Cretan
			pr.n., <u>GDI</u>iv<i><span class="en">
				p.1033
			</span></i>.</div>
